  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1139 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nDry conditions look to come to an end in the Mid-South right on\nschedule as we wrap up our climatologically driest month of the\nyear. Unfortunately, the arrival of rain coincides with Halloween. \n\nSurface high pressure over the Appalachians will weaken and shift\noff to the north today. Meanwhile, a deepening trough along the \nWest Coast will result in the amplification of a ridge over the \nPlains. The latter feature will expand today, extending into the \nlower Mississippi River Valley. As a result, dry conditions will \npersist and despite increasing cloud cover, temperatures will be \nas warm or slightly warmer than yesterday. Elevated fire weather\nconditions will continue today. Expect highs in the low 80s with\nafternoon RH in the upper 20s to low 30s. Late tonight into \nSaturday, the ridge will shift north and the trough along the west\ncoast will move onshore, resulting in strengthening southwest \nflow across the Mid-South. \n\nSouthern stream energy will result in our first period of widespread\nrainfall late Saturday and continue into Sunday. As the \nsubtropical jet strengthens over the Southwest, a shortwave will \napproach the Mid-South tonight. At the surface, southerly flow \nwill strengthen, slowly ushering moisture back into the region. It\nwill take 12 hours or so for appreciable moisture to make it into\nthe region but increasing high and midlevel clouds will be an \nindicator of things to come. After the initial main shortwave \ntonight or early Saturday, a series of weaker disturbances will \ncontinue to track out of the Southern Plains/Texas across the \nMid-South. Light rain should increase in coverage during the \nafternoon hours. Overnight, a strengthening jet and marginal \ninstability will result in heavier rainfall and maybe even a few \nembedded thunderstorms. The potential for heavier rain and \nthunderstorms will continue early Sunday, but diminish by midday. \nExpected rainfall totals late Saturday through Sunday are up to \none half of an inch in east Arkansas and the Missouri Bootheel, \nincreasing to up to an inch in northeast Mississippi and near the \nTennessee River in west Tennessee. \n\nA Colorado Low will slowly shift over the Plains early in the \nweek and approach the middle to lower Mississippi River Valley \nWednesday into Thursday. Consensus now has a main low moving into \nMinnesota early Tuesday, but a secondary low is expected farther, \napproaching the middle Mississippi River Valley Wednesday. Exact \nlocation and timing of the low remains uncertain, but a wetter \npattern is expected for most of the work week. Rainfall totals \nnext week have trended higher. The NBM now indicates widespread \n1.25 to 2.0 inch totals with up to 2.75 inches in the Delta area \nsouth of Memphis. Temperatures will remain seasonably warm next \nweek, featuring highs in the mid 70s and lows in the 50s and 60s.",
